---
Pricing an order line typed in at the till.

A line can be wrong in several ways, and the till has to say which. So each
step returns a `Result` with [OrderError] as its error, and the steps are
joined with `?` and the `Result` combinators.
---
commons till.order

uses till.menu

--- How many of one item a single line may ask for.
type Quantity = Int where InRange(1, 20)

---
Why an order line was refused.

A domain error type, not a `String`: each variant carries what the till needs
to explain the problem, and a `match` on it must cover every case.
---
type OrderError =
	| UnknownProduct(sku: String)
	| NotANumber(text: String)
	| OutOfRange(quantity: Int)

---
The quantity typed in, checked.

`Int.parse` returns an `Option`: it says only that the text was not a number,
and `okOr` turns that absence into the error we want to report. `Quantity.of`
fails with a `ValidationError`, which `mapErr` replaces with our own variant,
so every caller sees the one error type.
---
fn parseQuantity(text: String) -> Result[Quantity, OrderError] {
	let n = Int.parse(text).okOr(NotANumber(text))?
	Quantity.of(n).mapErr((invalid) => OutOfRange(n))
}

---
The price of one line, in pence.

Each `?` unwraps a success or returns the failure straight to the caller, so
the happy path reads top to bottom. An unknown SKU is absent from the menu,
which is an `Option`; here it is a mistake, so `okOr` makes it an error.
---
fn priceLine(sku: String, quantity: String) -> Result[Int, OrderError] {
	let product = findProduct(sku).okOr(UnknownProduct(sku))?
	let n = parseQuantity(quantity)?
	Ok(product.pence * n)
}

--- One line as it was typed in.
type Line = {
	sku: String,
	quantity: String,
}

---
The total for a whole order, before any discount.

`sequenceTry` stops at the first bad line, which is all the till needs to
ask the customer to fix it.
---
fn subtotal(lines: List[Line]) -> Result[Int, OrderError] {
	let prices = lines.map((l) => priceLine(l.sku, l.quantity)).sequenceTry()?
	Ok(prices.sum((p) => p))
}

---
What the customer pays: the subtotal, less any loyalty discount.

The card is optional and its absence is fine; a bad line is not, and its
error passes through unchanged.
---
fn total(lines: List[Line], card: Option[String]) -> Result[Int, OrderError] {
	subtotal(lines).map((pence) => pence - pence * discountFor(card) / 100)
}

---
A price for the customer display, which shows a price or nothing.

The display has no room for a reason, so `ok()` drops the error and keeps
an `Option`.
---
fn preview(sku: String, quantity: String) -> Option[Int] {
	priceLine(sku, quantity).ok()
}

--- What the till says about a refused line.
fn explain(error: OrderError) -> String {
	match error {
		UnknownProduct(sku) => "we don't sell \(sku)",
		NotANumber(text) => "\"\(text)\" is not a number",
		OutOfRange(n) => "\(n) is not between 1 and 20",
	}
}

---
The receipt text for one line.

`let ... else match` binds the price when the line is good. When it is not,
the `else match` arm sees the error and its value becomes the result instead.
---
fn receiptLine(sku: String, quantity: String) -> String {
	let Ok(pence) = priceLine(sku, quantity) else match {
		Err(error) => "refused: \(explain(error))",
	}
	"\(quantity) x \(sku): \(pence)p"
}
